Understanding Terraform State & Remote Backends 

When you terraform apply, Terraform creates a state file (terraform.tfstate) that keeps track of your infrastructure. This state is critical—it's how Terraform knows what exists and what needs to change.

But here’s the challenge 
  -->What happens when you're working in a team?
  -->How do you avoid overwriting each other’s changes?
 --> How do you secure this state file?

That's where Remote Backends come in!

Instead of storing the state locally, you can configure Terraform to use a remote backend like:
Ø AWS S3 (with DynamoDB for state locking)
Ø Azure Blob Storage
Ø  Google Cloud Storage
Ø  Terraform Cloud

This brings:
o  Team Collaboration
o  Security
o  Locking & Consistency


Example:-

 Use this block to configure S3 backend:

terraform {
 backend "s3" {
   bucket        = "my-terraform-state"
   key           = "dev/terraform.tfstate"
   region        = "us-east-1"
   dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
   encrypt       = true
 }
}
